In short: you may clone it and run it for your own learning. You may not
publish, copy, host, sell or reuse it, or present it as your own. Changes
happen only through the Owner: first an issue the Owner approves, then a
pull request the Owner approves.

1. DEFINITIONS

   "Software" means everything in this repository: source code, text,
   explanations, diagrams, data, styles, images and documentation, in any
   version.

   "Owner" means Haidar Hammoud (GitHub: @haidarhmd1).

   "Official Repository" means
   https://github.com/haidarhmd1/scalestation-system-design.

   "Contribution" means any change you propose to the Software through the
   process in section 4.

2. WHAT YOU MAY DO

   Subject to this license, you may:

   a) view and read the Software;

   b) clone or download the Software to your own device and run it locally,
      for your own personal, non-commercial learning;

   c) change your local copy privately, only to try things out or to
      prepare a Contribution that the Owner has approved under section 4;

   d) create a fork on GitHub only as far as needed to submit an approved
      Contribution as a pull request to the Official Repository.

3. WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO

   Unless the Owner has given you prior written permission, you may not:

   a) copy, reproduce, publish, republish, mirror or redistribute the
      Software or any part of it, in original or modified form, in any
      medium or on any platform;

   b) publish, maintain or distribute a fork or modified version, or use a
      fork for anything other than submitting an approved pull request;

   c) create, publish or distribute derivative works based on the Software,
      including translations, ports, re-skins or "inspired" copies that
      reuse its code, text, explanations, diagrams or data;

   d) host, deploy or make the Software available to others as a website,
      app or service;

   e) use the Software, or any part of it, for any commercial purpose,
      including paid courses, products, consulting or training material;

   f) sell, sublicense, rent or lend the Software;

   g) use the Software, or any part of it, to train, fine-tune or evaluate
      machine-learning or AI models, or include it in datasets;

   h) remove or change any copyright notice, this license, or attribution;

   i) present the Software or any part of it as your own work, or use the
      name "ScaleStation", the Owner's name or any branding to endorse or
      promote anything.

4. CONTRIBUTIONS

   Changes to the Software are made only through the Owner, in two steps:

   a) Issue first. Before writing a change, open an issue in the Official
      Repository describing what you want to change and why. Work on a
      pull request may only start after the Owner has approved the issue.
      Small fixes (typos, broken links) may skip this step, but the Owner
      may still close any pull request that has no approved issue.

   b) Pull request second. Submit the approved change as a pull request to
      the Official Repository, linked to its issue. The Owner reviews it
      and decides alone and at their sole discretion whether to accept,
      change or reject it. Nothing is merged without the Owner's approval,
      and the Owner has no obligation to accept any Contribution.

   c) By submitting a Contribution you confirm that it is your own original
      work, or that you have the right to submit it, and you grant the Owner
      a perpetual, worldwide, irrevocable, royalty-free, non-exclusive
      license to use, copy, modify, relicense and distribute your
      Contribution as part of the Software or otherwise. You receive no
      payment and no ownership of the Software.

   d) A fork created to prepare a pull request may be used for nothing else.
      Delete it, or keep it private to yourself, once your pull request is
      merged or closed.

5. FORKS ON GITHUB

   Because the Official Repository is public on GitHub, GitHub's Terms of
   Service allow GitHub users to view and fork it within GitHub. This
   license does not take away that platform function, but it limits what
   you may do with a fork: section 3 applies to every fork, and a fork may
   only be used as described in section 4. A fork used in any other way
   infringes the Owner's copyright and may be removed through a takedown
   request to GitHub.

